   summary="The meeting considered information from the Department of Land Transport and the Electronic Transactions Development Agency (ETDA). App-based car hire comes under a 2564 ministerial regulation issued under the Motor Vehicle Act 2522. There are 8,740 correctly registered app-based hire cars (Ror Yor 18) and 11,647 public motorcycles serving through apps (Ror Yor 17), 20,387 in all. Reasons some drivers have not registered include hire-purchase fees and interest, higher commercial insurance premiums than for private cars, and the resale price of vehicles with public plates. The Department of Land Transport has worked with the Bank of Thailand and the Office of Insurance Commission (OIC) on hire-purchase contracts and insurance models. The Ministry of Transport has proposed amending the ministerial regulation so that companies can register Ror Yor 18 cars for drivers to rent, and to raise the engine limit for public motorcycles from 125 to 250 cubic centimetres for set models. ETDA said 5 platform operators are linked to the ThaID identity system, and from 1 November 2566 to 30 September 2569 there were 241 complaints, about matters such as job allocation, pay, platform fees and safety. The subcommittee suggested lowering drivers' costs to encourage registration, checking drivers' records and qualifications from time to time, a QR code so passengers can easily check the vehicle and driver, studying extra insurance with platform operators taking part, and looking at the fairness of platform fees (GP). The next meeting will hear about law enforcement, safety, insurance and compensation for accident victims.",
